# eBike project — custom Kconfig options.

mainmenu "eBike firmware"

# === AWS IoT cloud link ======================================================

config EBIKE_PROVISION_CERTS
	bool "Provision the AWS certificates into the modem at boot"
	default y
	depends on MODEM_KEY_MGMT
	help
	  Embeds the root CA, device certificate and private key (PEM files
	  from firmware/certs/, not versioned) in the image and writes them
	  into the modem under CONFIG_MQTT_HELPER_SEC_TAG at startup, only when
	  missing or different. Development only: the credentials are then
	  readable in the flash image. Disable in production and provision out
	  of band.

config EBIKE_PROVISION_FORCE
	bool "Rewrite the AWS credentials even when already present"
	default n
	depends on EBIKE_PROVISION_CERTS
	help
	  The private key, and on some modem firmware the device certificate,
	  cannot be read back (AT error 514): only their existence can be
	  checked, never their contents. The boot therefore keeps whatever is
	  already stored in the modem.

	  Enable this to rotate them: the credentials are rewritten at every
	  boot whatever is stored. Turn it back off once the new PEM files are
	  in, otherwise every boot writes to the modem NVM for nothing.

config EBIKE_PUBLISH_PARKED_S
	int "Publish interval while parked (seconds)"
	default 600
	range 30 86400
	help
	  Interval between MQTT publications when the bike is stationary. A
	  reduced rate means fewer LTE wake-ups (PSM becomes possible) and less
	  cellular data. Theft alerts still leave immediately, outside the
	  cycle.

config EBIKE_TRANSITION_DEBOUNCE_S
	int "Debounce of transition publications (seconds)"
	default 20
	range 0 300
	help
	  A state transition triggers an immediate publication. Within this
	  delay of the previous one it is postponed instead of sent right away:
	  four transitions in 105 s once produced eight off-cycle publications
	  and left the GNSS without an RF window for minutes. 0 disables it.

config EBIKE_SHADOW_REPORT_MIN_S
	int "Minimum interval between two shadow reports (seconds)"
	default 30
	range 0 3600
	help
	  Same idea as the debounce above, applied to `shadow/update`: a report
	  too close to the previous one is held back and leaves with the next
	  publication. The reported state stays correct, it just arrives
	  grouped.

config EBIKE_SHADOW_POLL_S
	int "Periodic Device Shadow re-read (seconds, 0 = disabled)"
	default 300
	range 0 86400
	help
	  The device publishes a `shadow/get` at this rate, during a
	  publication cycle. A shadow delta is a DOWNLINK message, and with PSM
	  the bike is only reachable in the short window following its own
	  transmissions, so a command can otherwise wait several minutes. A
	  `get` is a PULL: the answer comes back while the RRC connection is
	  still open, which bounds the wait to this period. Cost: get/accepted
	  returns the full document with metadata, about 1 KB.

config EBIKE_PARK_TIMEOUT_S
	int "Stillness delay before entering PARKED (seconds)"
	default 120
	range 10 3600
	help
	  Time with no movement at all (neither wheel pulse nor IMU motion)
	  after which the state machine goes from RIDING to PARKED. Avoids
	  flipping states at a red light.

config EBIKE_ALERT_QUEUE_SIZE
	int "Depth of the alert queue"
	default 8
	range 1 32
	help
	  Alerts are published off-cycle and used to be simply LOST when the
	  MQTT link was down. They now all go through this queue, drained on
	  the cloud workqueue — sent immediately when the link is up, replayed
	  on reconnection otherwise, carrying their age (`age_s`) so the e-mail
	  dates them correctly.

	  The queue is not only an offline buffer: it also moves the TLS send
	  out of the emitter's context. A geofence alert is raised by a zbus
	  listener running in the SYSTEM workqueue, where publishing MQTT
	  blocked GNSS frame processing and the speed ticker.

	  When full, the OLDEST is overwritten: in a burst of geofence
	  reminders the most recent ones describe better where the bike is.

config EBIKE_BACKLOG_BATCHES_PER_CYCLE
	int "Catch-up batches sent per publication cycle"
	default 4
	range 1 32
	help
	  The trace buffer is drained in batches of 16 points. This bounds how
	  many batches leave per cycle: flushing 20 minutes of backlog at once
	  would hold the radio for tens of seconds and starve the GNSS.

	  FLOOR: the drain must exceed steady-state PRODUCTION, otherwise the
	  buffer grows until saturation. Production per cycle = publish rate /
	  sampling rate, so 12 points while riding and 10 while parked. One
	  batch is therefore enough in both states, and this bound only affects
	  catch-up after an outage, which was its original intent.

config EBIKE_CLOUD_BACKOFF_MIN_S
	int "Initial cloud reconnection delay (seconds)"
	default 5
	range 1 3600
	help
	  First delay before retrying a broker connection after a failure.
	  Doubled on each failure up to EBIKE_CLOUD_BACKOFF_MAX_S, and reset to
	  this value as soon as a connection succeeds.

config EBIKE_CLOUD_BACKOFF_MAX_S
	int "Maximum cloud reconnection delay (seconds)"
	default 300
	range 10 86400
	help
	  Cap of the exponential backoff. Retrying every 30 s forever burns
	  energy for nothing in a prolonged dead zone, since each attempt wakes
	  the radio. A jitter of +/-25% keeps the attempts from resynchronising
	  onto the publication cycle.

config EBIKE_CLOUD_MAX_CONNECT_FAILURES
	int "Consecutive connection failures before restarting (0 = never)"
	default 10
	range 0 100
	help
	  Beyond this many CONSECUTIVE aws_iot_connect() failures, the board
	  restarts.

	  WHY THIS LAST RESORT. After an abrupt RF loss, every reconnection can
	  fail with -ENOMEM — the signature of a TLS socket never released on
	  the modem side, whose pool is then exhausted. The bike goes mute for
	  good, retrying a call that can no longer succeed, and there is no
	  clean way to force the cleanup: mqtt_helper_disconnect() requires the
	  CONNECTED state. Only a reboot recovers the resource.

	  WHAT IT DOES NOT TRIGGER: a plain dead zone. Without LTE attached the
	  firmware does not even ATTEMPT a connection, so the counter only
	  advances when the network is there and the broker stays unreachable
	  — the genuinely abnormal case.

	  COST: the RAM trace buffer is lost on reboot. Acceptable, because by
	  the time the threshold is reached the link has been dead longer than
	  the buffer depth anyway. The restart is traceable: `boot` = "warm N"
	  with an empty `crash`, and `reset` = "software".

config EBIKE_CLOUD_STACK_SIZE
	int "Cloud workqueue stack (bytes)"
	default 4096
	range 2048 16384
	help
	  connect_work (DNS plus TLS handshake) and publish_work (JSON build
	  plus send) run on this DEDICATED workqueue rather than the system
	  one, for two reasons: a slow TLS handshake used to block GNSS PVT
	  processing behind it, and the watchdog can now watch the system
	  workqueue with a tight timeout without risking a spurious reboot
	  during a legitimately slow reconnection.

# === Cellular connectivity ===================================================

config EBIKE_APN
	string "SIM APN (empty = APN supplied by the network)"
	default ""
	help
	  Access Point Name forced on the default PDP context (CID 0). Leave
	  empty to let the network provide it, which is what most LTE-M IoT
	  SIMs expect. Requires CONFIG_LTE_LC_PDN_MODULE=y.

config EBIKE_PDN_IPV4_ONLY
	bool "Request an IPv4-only default PDP context"
	default n
	help
	  Diagnostic fallback: ask for IPv4 instead of IPv4v6, as some visited
	  networks reject the IPV4V6 type. No effect when EBIKE_APN is empty.

# === Status LEDs =============================================================

config EBIKE_STATUS_LED
	bool "Status LEDs on LED1 and LED2"
	default y
	depends on GPIO
	help
	  On battery the DK has no console: its UART goes through the interface
	  MCU, which needs a USB host. The application LEDs are the only
	  observable output left, and nothing else in the firmware uses them.

	  LED1 carries liveness and the GNSS (brief blip = running without a
	  fresh fix, even 1 Hz blink = running with one), LED2 carries the
	  connection ladder (dark = no LTE, blink = attached, solid = connected
	  to AWS IoT). That is enough to tell "the firmware is not running"
	  from "the firmware runs but the modem never attaches" — the two
	  failures that look identical on the dashboard.

	  Only LED1 and LED2 are used: all four LEDs sit on Arduino header pins
	  (LED1=D0, LED2=D1, LED3=D4, LED4=D5) and the X-NUCLEO-IKS01A1 shield
	  holds D4/D5 down, so LED3 and LED4 stay dark even during the lamp
	  test. See EBIKE_STATUS_LED_EXTENDED for a board without that shield.

	  Costs a few mA continuously: disable for endurance runs where the
	  diagnosis is no longer needed.

config EBIKE_STATUS_LED_EXTENDED
	bool "Also drive LED3 (cloud) and LED4 (GNSS fix)"
	default n
	depends on EBIKE_STATUS_LED
	help
	  Adds two dedicated LEDs: LED3 on while the MQTT connection is up,
	  LED4 on while the GNSS fix is fresh. They duplicate what LED1 and
	  LED2 already encode, in a form that needs no pattern reading.

	  Off by default because LED3/LED4 are Arduino D4/D5, which a mounted
	  IKS01A1 shield holds down: the firmware would be driving pins against
	  the shield for LEDs that never light.

config EBIKE_STATUS_LED_TICK_MS
	int "Status LED slot period (milliseconds)"
	default 100
	depends on EBIKE_STATUS_LED
	range 20 1000
	help
	  Duration of one slot of the blink patterns. A pattern is ten slots,
	  so 100 ms gives a one-second cycle: a 100 ms blip, or an even 1 Hz
	  blink — fast enough to read at a glance, slow enough to stay
	  unambiguous.

config EBIKE_STATUS_LED_LAMP_TEST_MS
	int "Lamp test duration at boot (milliseconds, 0 = none)"
	default 1000
	depends on EBIKE_STATUS_LED
	range 0 5000
	help
	  Every driven LED is lit together for this long when status_led_init()
	  runs, then the normal display takes over.

	  It is the earliest sign of life the board can give on battery, and it
	  tells a dead indicator from a false one: every LED sits on an Arduino
	  header pin, so a mounted shield can hold one down whatever the
	  firmware does. A LED that misses the lamp test is unusable, not a
	  state that never happened.

config EBIKE_STATUS_LED_FIX_FRESH_S
	int "Age beyond which the GNSS indication goes dark (seconds)"
	default 120
	depends on EBIKE_STATUS_LED
	range 5 3600
	help
	  The fix indication holds while the last valid fix is younger than
	  this. Beyond it the position is stale and the indication drops back —
	  it must mean "the bike knows where it is now", not "it knew once".

# === Watchdog ================================================================

config EBIKE_WDT
	bool "Hardware watchdog (task watchdog)"
	default y
	depends on TASK_WDT
	help
	  Fatal errors are already captured and reported on the next boot, but
	  a silent hang — a thread stuck on a socket or a mutex — produces no
	  fault at all and simply leaves the bike mute. The task watchdog
	  watches the loops that must run continuously and restarts the board
	  when one of them goes silent. The reset is reported on the next boot.

config EBIKE_WDT_TIMEOUT_S
	int "Watchdog timeout of the short loops (seconds)"
	default 30
	depends on EBIKE_WDT
	range 5 600
	help
	  Applies to the state machine loop (wakes at least every 5 s) and to
	  the system workqueue beat. Both only run bounded work, so 30 s is
	  already very generous.

config EBIKE_WDT_CLOUD_TIMEOUT_S
	int "Watchdog timeout of the cloud workqueue (seconds)"
	default 180
	depends on EBIKE_WDT
	range 30 3600
	help
	  Separate channel, deliberately permissive: aws_iot_connect() can
	  legitimately block for tens of seconds (DNS plus TLS handshake on a
	  degraded network). This bounds a DEFINITIVE hang of the cloud path
	  without punishing a slow reconnection.

config EBIKE_WDT_FEED_S
	int "Watchdog beat period (seconds)"
	default 5
	depends on EBIKE_WDT
	range 1 60
	help
	  Rate at which the channels are fed. Must stay well below the
	  shortest timeout above.

# === Geofence ================================================================

config EBIKE_GEOFENCE_FLIP_GUARD_S
	int "Minimum delay between two zone state reversals (seconds)"
	default 120
	range 0 3600
	help
	  TEMPORAL debounce, on top of the spatial hysteresis (re-entry at 90%
	  of the radius). The spatial guard is not enough when the bike is
	  parked right on the boundary: GNSS accuracy makes the distance
	  oscillate across the threshold, which produced four alerts in 82
	  seconds on a stationary bike. This delays REVERSALS only: a genuine
	  exit is not slowed down and the 60 s out-of-zone reminders continue.
	  0 disables the guard.

config EBIKE_GEOFENCE_EXIT_COUNT
	int "Consecutive out-of-zone fixes before a geofence alert"
	default 2
	range 1 20
	help
	  Number of consecutive fixes seen outside (distance minus fix accuracy
	  above the radius) before declaring an exit. Filters isolated position
	  jumps; in continuous navigation two fixes add about one second.

# === Shared runtime defaults (carried on CH_CONFIG) ==========================

config EBIKE_PUBLISH_RIDING_S
	int "Publish interval while riding (seconds)"
	default 60
	range 10 3600
	help
	  Interval between MQTT publications when the bike is moving. Below
	  60 s the LTE link stays in RRC connected almost permanently and
	  starves the GNSS receiver.

config EBIKE_MOTION_THRESHOLD_MG
	int "IMU motion detection threshold (milli-g)"
	default 150
	range 10 2000
	help
	  Norm of the acceleration delta between two IMU polls above which
	  movement is reported. Using the delta cancels gravity, so the test is
	  insensitive to how the bike is oriented. 150 mg ignores sensor noise
	  (~10-20 mg) while still catching someone handling the bike.

config EBIKE_GEOFENCE_RADIUS_M
	int "Default geofence radius (metres)"
	default 500
	range 20 1000000
	help
	  Allowed radius around the home point. A value pushed through the
	  Device Shadow and persisted in NVS takes precedence.

# === Wheel speed sensor ======================================================

config EBIKE_WHEEL_CIRC_MM
	int "Default wheel circumference (millimetres)"
	default 2200
	range 500 4000
	help
	  Circumference of the wheel carrying the magnet (26" is about 2075 mm,
	  700C/28" 2100-2250 mm depending on the tyre). A value persisted in
	  NVS takes precedence.

config EBIKE_SPEED_DEBOUNCE_MS
	int "Speed sensor debounce (milliseconds)"
	default 20
	range 1 200
	help
	  Window during which edges following a pulse are ignored (reed bounce
	  is typically under 2 ms). It also bounds the maximum measurable
	  speed: 20 ms allows about 380 km/h with a 2.1 m wheel and one magnet.

config EBIKE_SPEED_STOP_TIMEOUT_S
	int "Wheel stopped timeout (seconds)"
	default 3
	range 1 30
	help
	  With no pulse for this long the speed drops to zero. 3 s means the
	  lowest detectable speed is around 2.5 km/h with a 2.1 m wheel.

# === GNSS ====================================================================

config EBIKE_GNSS_FIX_INTERVAL
	int "Interval between GNSS fixes (seconds, 1 = continuous)"
	default 1
	range 1 65535
	help
	  Passed to nrf_modem_gnss_fix_interval_set(). 1 means continuous
	  navigation (one PVT per second, instant re-fix in every free RF
	  window), which is what the batched sampling below expects. Values
	  between 2 and 9 are rejected by the modem.

config EBIKE_GNSS_FIX_RETRY
	int "Maximum duration of one fix attempt (seconds, 0 = unlimited)"
	default 0
	help
	  Time allowed to each periodic fix attempt. 0 lets the receiver search
	  without limit, which is what a cold start needs to download
	  ephemerides.

config EBIKE_TELEMETRY_SAMPLE_S
	int "Position sampling period (seconds)"
	default 5
	range 1 60
	help
	  Rate at which the last valid GNSS position is appended to the trace
	  buffer (only when a new fix arrived since the previous sample). The
	  buffer is drained and published as one grouped MQTT message per
	  supervision cycle.

config EBIKE_TELEMETRY_SAMPLE_PARKED_S
	int "Position sampling period while parked (seconds)"
	default 60
	range 1 3600
	help
	  Replaces EBIKE_TELEMETRY_SAMPLE_S in the PARKED state only; RIDING
	  and ALARM keep the fine rate.

	  A parked bike has not moved for EBIKE_PARK_TIMEOUT_S by construction,
	  so recording its position every 5 s produced 120 identical points per
	  cycle that then had to be flushed as eight consecutive MQTT messages.
	  The backlog catch-up, designed for outages, had become the permanent
	  regime of parking. At 60 s a parked cycle holds ten points and one
	  batch, with no functional loss: any movement switches back to RIDING
	  (or ALARM) and restores the fine rate immediately.

config EBIKE_TRACE_RING_SIZE
	int "Capacity of the GNSS trace buffer (samples)"
	default 240
	range 16 2048
	help
	  Position samples kept in RAM between two successful publications.
	  OFFLINE this is the depth of saved history: beyond it the oldest
	  points are overwritten and lost. Coverage = size x current sampling
	  rate, so 240 samples hold 20 min while riding and 4 h while parked.
	  Cost: 32 bytes per sample, 7.5 KB of RAM at the default size.

config EBIKE_GNSS_MAX_ACC_M
	int "Horizontal accuracy above which a fix is rejected (metres)"
	default 150
	range 20 10000
	help
	  Guard against clearly bad solutions: a fix less precise than this is
	  ignored entirely (no trace, no geofence, no last known fix).
	  Deliberately wide — the point is to reject garbage, not to filter on
	  quality. In continuous navigation, rejecting a fix costs nothing.

config EBIKE_GNSS_MAX_SPEED_KMH
	int "GNSS speed above which the fix speed is ignored (km/h)"
	default 60
	range 10 500
	help
	  The modem instantaneous Doppler speed is noisy at low speed and can
	  report several times the real value on an otherwise well located fix.
	  Above this threshold the speed is marked UNKNOWN (the `spd` field is
	  omitted from the point) while the POSITION is kept, since that is
	  what carries the trace.

config EBIKE_GNSS_MAX_SPEED_ACC_KMH
	int "Tolerated speed uncertainty (km/h, 0 = filter disabled)"
	default 0
	range 0 200
	help
	  Complementary statistical filter based on `speed_accuracy`, the
	  1-sigma figure the modem attaches to every PVT. DISABLED by default
	  on purpose: calibrating it blind risks discarding nearly every speed.
	  The status publishes the observed uncertainty (`sacc`, in 0.1 km/h)
	  so the threshold can be set from real data.

config EBIKE_AGNSS
	bool "A-GNSS assistance from nRF Cloud (CoAP)"
	default y
	depends on NRF_CLOUD_COAP && NRF_CLOUD_AGNSS
	help
	  Answers modem assistance requests (NRF_MODEM_GNSS_EVT_AGNSS_REQ) by
	  downloading ephemerides, almanac, time and position from nRF Cloud
	  over CoAP. This is a one-shot fetch, independent of the AWS link, and
	  it cuts the cold TTFF from minutes to seconds. Requires the board to
	  be onboarded on nRF Cloud with credentials in
	  CONFIG_NRF_CLOUD_SEC_TAG. Without them the fetch fails cleanly and
	  GNSS falls back to an autonomous search.

config EBIKE_AGNSS_STACK_SIZE
	int "A-GNSS workqueue stack (bytes)"
	default 4096
	depends on EBIKE_AGNSS
	help
	  The fetch (CoAP/DTLS connection plus transfer) blocks, so it runs on
	  its own workqueue instead of freezing the system one, which handles
	  GNSS PVT frames and telemetry publication.

# === IMU =====================================================================

config EBIKE_IMU_POLL_MS
	int "Accelerometer polling period (milliseconds)"
	default 1000
	range 20 60000
	help
	  The lsm6ds0 driver has no trigger support, so the accelerometer is
	  polled. Do not go below the sensor ODR
	  (CONFIG_LSM6DS0_ACCEL_SAMPLING_RATE, 10 Hz by default, i.e. 100 ms).

module = EBIKE
module-str = eBike
source "${ZEPHYR_BASE}/subsys/logging/Kconfig.template.log_config"

source "Kconfig.zephyr"
